[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin xyplot n 1.0.1]
[see_also Plotchart]
[keywords plotting xy-plot zooming]
[moddesc   Plotchart]
[titledesc {Interactive XY plot widget}]
[require Tcl [opt 8.5]]
[require Tk [opt 8.5]]
[require xyplot [opt 1.0.1]]
[description]

The [package xyplot] package provides a widget showing one or more
series of xy data as a [package Plotchart] xy-plot, with scrollbars
and zooming with the mouse.  The axes are scaled to the data
automatically, and the plot is redrawn when the data change or the
widget is resized.

[section COMMANDS]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd xyplot] [arg pathName] [opt [arg options]]]

Creates the widget [arg pathName] (a frame holding a canvas and two
scrollbars) and a command of the same name, and returns
[arg pathName].  The options are:

[list_begin options]

[opt_def -title [arg text]]
The title of the plot.

[opt_def -xtext [arg text]]
[opt_def -ytext [arg text]]
The texts of the x- and y-axis.

[opt_def -xticks [arg number]]
[opt_def -yticks [arg number]]
The number of intervals between the tick marks on the x- and y-axis
(default 10).  A grid is drawn at the tick marks.

[opt_def -xformat [arg format]]
[opt_def -yformat [arg format]]
The [cmd format] specification of the labels of the x- and y-axis.

[opt_def -xinteger [arg boolean]]
[opt_def -yinteger [arg boolean]]
If true, a zoomed view of the x- or y-axis starts at an integer
value and spans a multiple of the number of ticks, so that the tick
marks are at integer values (default 0).

[opt_def -type [arg type]]
How the data are drawn: [const line] (default) connects the points of
a series with lines, [const dot] draws a dot at each point.

[opt_def -background [arg color]]
The background color of the plot area.

[opt_def -canvasbackground [arg color]]
The background color around the plot area (axes, title).

[opt_def -ttk [arg boolean]]
If true, the frame and the scrollbars are [cmd ttk] widgets
(default 0).

[list_end]
[list_end]

[section {WIDGET COMMAND}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [arg pathName] [method add_data] [arg series] [arg xydata] [opt [arg options]]]

Adds the data series [arg series] and returns its name.
[arg xydata] is a flat list of x and y values,
[arg x1] [arg y1] [arg x2] [arg y2] ...  The options are:

[list_begin options]
[opt_def -color [arg color]]
The color of the series.
[opt_def -legend [arg text]]
The text for the series in the legend.  Only series with a legend text
are shown in the legend.
[list_end]

[call [arg pathName] [method set_data] [arg series] [arg xydata]]

Replaces the data of the existing series [arg series].

[call [arg pathName] [method remove_data] [arg series]]

Removes the series [arg series].

[call [arg pathName] [method cget] [arg what] [opt [arg series]]]

Returns information about the widget.  [arg what] is one of:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const canvas]] The canvas widget the plot is drawn in.
[def "[const data] [arg series]"] The data of [arg series], as given to
[method add_data] or [method set_data].
[def [const series]] The list of the names of the series.
[def [const scale]] The list [arg "xmin xmax ymin ymax"] of the
ranges of all data, followed by [arg "xmin xmax ymin ymax"] of the
part shown.
[def [const xyplot]] The [package Plotchart] xy-plot command of the
current plot, for further changes with the methods described in
[package Plotchart].  The plot is created anew whenever it is redrawn,
so such changes are lost then.
[def [const zoomstack]] The list of the zoomed views, see
[method set_zoomstack].
[list_end]

[call [arg pathName] [method set_zoomstack] [arg stack]]

Sets the list of zoomed views, as returned by [method cget]
[const zoomstack].  Each view is a list [arg "xmin xmax ymin ymax"].
Zooming in appends the new view to the list; unzooming removes the
last view and shows the one before it, or the whole range of the data
if there is none.  Setting the list does not change the view shown.

[list_end]

The changes made by [method add_data], [method set_data] and
[method remove_data] are shown when the event loop becomes idle.  The
widget is destroyed with [cmd destroy].

[section {MOUSE BINDINGS}]

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const Button-1]]
Dragging with button 1 draws a rectangle; when the button is released,
the plot zooms in to the part inside it.  The scrollbars then move the
view over the whole range of the data.
[def [const Button-3]]
Returns to the previous zoomed view, or to the whole range of the data.
[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example {
package require xyplot

set sine {}
set cosine {}
for {set i 0} {$i <= 100} {incr i} {
    set x [expr {$i / 10.0}]
    lappend sine   $x [expr {sin($x)}]
    lappend cosine $x [expr {cos($x)}]
}

set p [xyplot .p -title "Sine and cosine" -xtext x -ytext y \
           -xformat %.1f -yformat %.1f]
pack $p -fill both -expand true
$p add_data sine   $sine   -color red  -legend sin
$p add_data cosine $cosine -color blue -legend cos
}]

